   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to the imposition of sales and compensating use taxes in Putnam county",
    "basePrintNo": "S5761",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5761-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1210, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5761\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to the imposition of sales\nand compensating use taxes in Putnam county\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis bill would extend the current authorization of Putnam County to\nenact an additional one percent rate of sales and compensating use\ntax.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nThis bill would amend Section 1210 of the Tax Law, an amended by\nChapter 272 of the laws of 2007, to extend the current authorization\nfor Putnam County to enact an additional one percent sales and\ncompensating use tax from November 30, 2009 (its present sunset date)\nuntil November 30, 2011.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe Putnam County Executive and Board of Legislators have requested by\nCounty Resolution the authorization to continue to enact a local law\nto impose sales tax collection by one additional percent.\n\nChapter 272 of the laws of 2007 authorized the County of Putnam to\nimpose an additional 1% above the authorized 3% sales and use tax (so\nas to total 8 percent - 4 percent state and 4 percent local).  This\ncurrent 2007 authorization is scheduled to sunset on November 30,\n2009. This bill would simply extend the current authorization until\nNovember 30, 2011. This bill would NOT authorize a new sales tax, but\nmerely continue the existing authorization, for two additional years.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nAccording to Putnam County, the additional one percent sales tax\nauthorized by this bill would generate $12.9 million dollars that\nwould otherwise need to be collected by real property taxes.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act would take effect immediately.\n",
